The Nautilus DevOps team is testing various Ansible modules on servers in Stratos DC. They're currently focusing on file creation on remote hosts using Ansible. Here are the details:
a. Create an inventory file ~/playbook/inventory on jump host and include all app servers.
b. Create a playbook ~/playbook/playbook.yml to create a blank file /opt/nfsdata.txt on all app servers.
c. Set the permissions of the /opt/nfsdata.txt file to 0777.
d. Ensure the user/group owner of the /opt/nfsdata.txt file is tony on app server 1, steve on app server 2 and banner on app server 3.

Note: Validation will execute the playbook using the command ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml, so ensure the playbook functions correctly without any additional arguments.

SOLUTION

Yes. For this task, the important part is that each app server uses its own login user, and the playbook must run without requiring extra -e variables.

Use this setup on the jump host.

1. Create the directory
mkdir -p ~/playbook
cd ~/playbook
2. Create inventory
nano ~/playbook/inventory

Use:

[app]
stapp01 ansible_user=tony
stapp02 ansible_user=steve
stapp03 ansible_user=banner

If your lab requires SSH passwords and they are not already configured, add the corresponding ansible_password values supplied by the Stratos DC credentials/wiki. Don't guess those passwords.

You can test connectivity with:

ansible -i ~/playbook/inventory app -m ping

You should get SUCCESS for all three servers.

3. Create the playbook
nano ~/playbook/playbook.yml

Put this in it:

---
- name: Create nfsdata file on all app servers
  hosts: app
  become: yes


  tasks:
    - name: Create blank nfsdata file
      ansible.builtin.file:
        path: /opt/nfsdata.txt
        state: touch
        mode: '0777'
        owner: "{{ ansible_user }}"
        group: "{{ ansible_user }}"

This is a nice way to handle the different owners because:

stapp01 connects as tony → owner tony
stapp02 connects as steve → owner steve
stapp03 connects as banner → owner banner

become: yes allows Ansible to create the file under /opt.

4. Run exactly as the validator will
cd ~/playbook
ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml

You should see something similar to:

PLAY RECAP
stapp01    : ok=2    changed=1    unreachable=0    failed=0
stapp02    : ok=2    changed=1    unreachable=0    failed=0
stapp03    : ok=2    changed=1    unreachable=0    failed=0
5. Verify the result

You can check each server:

ansible -i ~/playbook/inventory app -m shell -a "ls -l /opt/nfsdata.txt"

Expected ownership:

-rwxrwxrwx ... tony   tony   /opt/nfsdata.txt
-rwxrwxrwx ... steve  steve  /opt/nfsdata.txt
-rwxrwxrwx ... banner banner /opt/nfsdata.txt

One important point: if ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml fails with Permission denied during SSH, don't change the playbook yet. That means the ansible_user/authentication details in inventory need fixing first.
